WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.969
This is the reason Speakeasy

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:03.970
with Nicolas Gillespie.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.619
All right. Well, thank you for

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.879
coming out.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.919
We are recording in Midtown

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.419
at the Blue Building, which is also

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.469
the home of the psychedelic

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.819
assembly.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.769
And we are talking

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.749
tonight with the authors of

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.299
the Canceling of the American Mind,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.399
how Cancel culture Undermines

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.379
trust, destroys institutions

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.999
and threatens us all.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.499
But there is a solution.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.599
I don't know about that subtitle,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.099
but we have the authors.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.779
They will explain it along with

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.909
everything else about this strange

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.229
world we find ourselves in.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.479
Greg Luciano and Ricky Shallot.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.480
Please welcome them.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.009
Greg, as I suspect

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.229
many of you know, is the

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.029
head of FIRE, the Foundation for

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.310
Individual Rights and Expression.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.719
And he's also the co-author

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.729
of a previous best selling book

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.379
called The Coddling of the American

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.380
Mind.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.169
And his coauthor this time

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.759
is Ricky Shallot, who's a New York

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.739
City based journalist who was a

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:32.740
fellow at

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.729
FIRE researcher and

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.579
as a New York Post columnist,

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.729
is a host of the Lost Debate

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.559
podcast and also at times

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.529
of Reason contributor.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.289
So please give it up for Greg and

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.290
Ricky.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.349
Okay, So let's start with the

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.519
subtitle.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.599
Okay. The book is called The

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.459
Canceling of the American Mind.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.899
And then the subtitle is Cancel

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.499
Culture Undermines Trust, Destroys

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.379
Institution and Threatens us

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.380
All...

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.919
But there is a solution.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.059
Yeah. Okay.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.379
There was some debate about that.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.759
I wanted an emoticon in there.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.679
Okay. You know that I might have

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.599
kept the sentence going, but why

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.039
don't we start?

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.749
Ricky, what is the what's the

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.609
elevator pitch for this book?

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.379
What is it about and why is it

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.339
relevant now?

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.869
Well, I think it's two-fold.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.729
On the first front, people are still

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:27.959
saying that cancel culture does not

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.849
exist, which is absolutely crazy and

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.679
defies all statistics

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:32.789
fundamentally.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.649
But also, cancel culture

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:35.819
is not just about the people that

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:36.929
are torn down, it's about the

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.969
epistemic crisis that it creates and

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.859
the devastation of the body

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.059
of common knowledge that we all

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.189
share and and also the undermining

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.809
of trust in between people

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.979
interpersonally.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.779
And for me, as a young person, the

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.759
undermining of of

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.019
being able to grow up and have the

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.729
freedom to fumble and make mistakes

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.109
as well. So I think it's important

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.609
on a ton of different levels.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.499
I have to say, as an old person,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.739
yeah, you know, tough.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:02.969
You should have been born when I was

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.650
born, but would you

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.859
agree? What is the working

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.659
definition of cancel culture?

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.789
It's basically we're trying to

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.529
give it the name of this historical

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:14.429
era that we're in.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.749
I'm a First Amendment lawyer.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.789
I'm big on the history of freedom

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.409
of speech and a lot of what we call

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.629
mass censorship events have names.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.879
So alien sedition in 1798,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.229
even those really Sedition Act of

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.419
1790, the Red Scare one

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.219
TS Red Scare two also known as

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.809
McCarthyism, etc.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.699
PMRC, the

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.629
comic book scare.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.309
So basically we're proposing more or

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.389
less that this be historical

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.309
definition of a unique and weird

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:39.929
period where there's been a lot of

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.849
people losing their jobs because of

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.559
their opinion. That's really one of

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:44.579
the things we're trying to show is

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.559
this is on par with any

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.299
of these previous moments of mass

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.009
censorship and actually exceeds them

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.329
in terms of numbers of professors

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:51.719
fired.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:52.979
Yeah. Talk a bit about the

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.179
professors, because, I mean, there

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:55.679
are a couple of different sites,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.299
right, or locations where this is

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.943
particularly strong academia,

00:03:58.944 --> 00:04:00.719
clearly one of them.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.889
What are the numbers that you're

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:02.429
talking about?

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.289
Yeah, so this was an

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:05.399
interesting thing. We now have a

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.959
great research Department of Fire

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.729
and that was, you know, partially a

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.109
labor of love on my part because I

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.759
was always the lawyer who was

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.049
pretending to be fire a social

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.489
scientist.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:15.689
But we've been able to grow it.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:16.619
That's how we actually have our

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.969
campus free speech rankings, which

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.739
is based on 13 different factors.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.179
So real quick, through the stats

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.049
and so and our definition is

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:26.939
the the uptick of

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.979
campaigns beginning around 2014

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.779
to get people fired, de-platformed,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.609
expelled, and and the

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.289
culture of fear that resulted from

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.909
that. And I think it's always

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.319
important to root numbers in

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.339
comparisons.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.469
When I started at FIRE,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.419
I started, I actually landed

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.379
in Philadelphia at 9:10

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.170
a.m. on 9/11,

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.589
all all of my first cases were

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.419
involving people who said jerky

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.519
or insensitive things about

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.439
the attacks or people

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.459
who said, Let's go get those

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:57.670
terrorists, but said it in a

00:04:58.680 --> 00:04:59.820
considered to be rude way.

